\\\n\\\\nFrom clue 6: **Hana sat next to Felix**  \\\\n→ |Hana - Felix| = 1\\\\n\\\\nSo Hana and Uma are both adjacent to Felix. That means Hana and Uma are on opposite sides of Felix, or possibly both on the same side? But since they are both adjacent to Felix, and there are only two adjacent seats, Hana and Uma must be on **opposite sides** of Felix, unless they are the same person (which they aren't). So:\\\\n\\\\n- Felix is between Hana and Uma, or\\\\n- Hana and Uma are on either side of Felix.\\\\n\\\\nSo the three are in a block: **Hana - Felix - Uma** or **Uma - Felix - Hana**\\\\n\\\\nBut wait: Hana and Uma are both adjacent to Felix, so the three must be in a sequence like:\\\\n\\\\n- Hana, Felix, Uma → Hana-Felix-Uma\\\\n- Uma, Felix, Hana → Uma-Felix-Hana\\\\n\\\\nSo **Felix is in the middle**, and Hana and Uma are on either side.\\\\n\\\\nSo we have a **triplet**: Hana, Felix, Uma in some order, with Felix in the middle.\\\\n\\\\nSo possible arrangements for the triplet:\\\\n- Hana, Felix, Uma\\\\n- Uma, Felix, Hana\\\\n\\\\nBut we also have clue 3: **Felix sat somewhere between Hana and Cyrus**  \\\\n→ So Hana and Cyrus are on opposite sides of Felix.\\\\n\\\\nSo from clue 3: Hana < Felix < Cyrus or Cyrus < Felix < Hana\\\\n\\\\nBut from above, Hana is adjacent to Felix, and in the triplet, Hana is on one side of Felix.\\\\n\\\\nSo if Hana is on the left of Felix, then for Felix to be between Hana and Cyrus, Cyrus must be on the right of Felix.\\\\n\\\\nSimilarly, if Hana is on the right of Felix, then Cyrus must be on the left.\\\\n\\\\nBut from the triplet, Hana and Uma are on opposite sides of Felix.\\\\n\\\\nSo let’s suppose:\\\\n\\\\n**Case A: Hana - Felix - Uma**  \\\\nThen Hana is left of Felix, Uma is right of Felix.\\\\n\\\\nFrom clue 3: Felix is between Hana and Cyrus → so Hana < Felix < Cyrus → so Cyrus must be to the right of Felix.\\\\n\\\\nSo Cyrus > Felix.\\\\n\\\\nAlso, from clue 5: **Cyrus sat somewhere between Uma and Vik**  \\\\n→ So